Using smbnetfs from pkgsrc
==========================

There is no kernel SMB client on NetBSD (mount_smbfs(8) was removed in
2020).  smbnetfs mounts SMB shares through FUSE, using NetBSD's native
refuse(3)/puffs(4) layer.

Configuration
-------------

smbnetfs reads its configuration from ~/.smb:

  $ mkdir ~/.smb
  $ cp @PKG_SYSCONFDIR@/samba/smb.conf ~/.smb/smb.conf
  $ cp @PREFIX@/share/examples/smbnetfs/smbnetfs.conf ~/.smb/smbnetfs.conf

Current Samba disables SMB1, so the network cannot be browsed.  Declare
each server in ~/.smb/smbnetfs.conf:

  host fileserver.example.org visible=true

Put the credentials in ~/.smb/smbnetfs.auth, which smbnetfs.conf
includes.  Use one line per share, or omit "/share" to use the same
login for every share on that host:

  auth "fileserver.example.org/share" "user" "password"

smbnetfs ignores host and auth lines unless the config files are
private:

  $ chmod 600 ~/.smb/smbnetfs.conf ~/.smb/smbnetfs.auth

Mounting
--------

To mount as an ordinary user, enable user mounts:

  # sysctl -w security.models.extensions.usermount=1

The shares appear as <mountpoint>/<host>/<share>:

  $ smbnetfs /mountpoint
  $ ls /mountpoint/fileserver.example.org/share

To debug, add "-o log_file=/tmp/smbnetfs.log,smbnetfs_debug=2".
df(1) shows 0 blocks unless "-o free_space_size=N" is given.

Unmounting
----------

A plain umount(8) can hang waiting for the smbnetfs daemon, and other
mount(8) calls then block as well.  Stop the daemon first:

  $ pkill -9 -f 'smbnetfs /mountpoint'; umount /mountpoint
